**1. Mindset Reset**

If any voice says “I should already know this basic thing”, replace it immediately with the diagnostic question only:

**“What specific prerequisite, assumption, or weak link is missing or shaky right now about how data flows from my component to the HTML template?”**

We treat any gap as useful information. The goal is to make interpolation automatic so it never sabotages later topics like property binding, change detection, or building reliable UI in your e-commerce product catalog.

**2. First-Principles Deconstruction**

Common assumptions / inherited ways of thinking about interpolation in Angular:

- “{{ }} is just a way to print variables in HTML like in some other frameworks.”
- “It’s the same as JavaScript template literals (`${}`) inside the HTML.”
- “I can put any JavaScript code inside {{ }} and it will work.”
- “It updates the DOM automatically because of magic two-way binding.”
- From tutorials: “Just use {{ propertyName }} and you’re done.”

Strip every assumption away.

What remains that is **fundamentally and provably true** at the lowest level?

- HTML by itself is static text — it cannot directly read values from JavaScript objects.
- Angular extends HTML with its own template syntax (compiled by Angular’s template compiler).
- **Interpolation** (`{{ expression }}`) is the simplest form of this syntax: Angular evaluates the expression in the context of the component’s TypeScript class, converts the result to a string, and inserts it as text content into the DOM.
- It is **one-way data binding** (component → view). Changes in the component property flow to the template. Changes in the DOM do not automatically flow back.
- The expression inside `{{ }}` is **not** full JavaScript. It is a safe, limited Angular expression (property access, method calls, basic operators, ternary, pipe usage — but no assignments, no `new`, no complex logic). This limitation is deliberate for security (no XSS from templates) and performance (easy to track for change detection).

Rebuilt from basics: Your component holds the data (TypeScript class properties/methods). The template is a blueprint that says “put the value of this expression here as text”. Angular’s change detection mechanism watches the component and re-evaluates the interpolation when needed, updating only the changed text nodes efficiently.

**What would someone need to question to genuinely understand instead of memorizing?**

- “What exactly gets evaluated and when — at build time or runtime?”
- “What happens if the expression returns an object or null?”
- “How does this differ from just writing plain text or other binding types like [property] or (event)?”
- “Why does Angular restrict what I can write inside {{ }}?”

**3. Concrete Examples as the Truth Detector**

Let’s work with real code from your e-commerce project.

**Example 1 – Basic property interpolation**

In your component TypeScript (`product-card.component.ts`):

```ts
export class ProductCardComponent {
  product: Product = {
    id: 1,
    name: 'Wireless Headphones',
    price: 2999,
    inStock: true
  };
}
```

In the template (`product-card.component.html`):

```html
<h3>{{ product.name }}</h3>
<p>Price: ₹{{ product.price }}</p>
<p>Status: {{ product.inStock ? 'In Stock' : 'Out of Stock' }}</p>
```

This evaluates `product.name`, converts to string, and puts it between the `<h3>` tags.

**Example 2 – Method call + calculation**

```ts
// In component
getDiscountedPrice(): number {
  return this.product.price * 0.9;  // 10% off
}
```

Template:
```html
<p>Discounted Price: ₹{{ getDiscountedPrice() }}</p>
<p>Total for 2 items: ₹{{ product.price * 2 }}</p>
```

**Example 3 – Edge cases (truth detector)**

```html
<!-- Safe handling of null/undefined -->
<p>{{ product.description || 'No description available' }}</p>

<!-- Pipe (built-in formatting) -->
<p>Price: {{ product.price | currency:'INR':'symbol':'1.0-0' }}</p>

<!-- What happens with objects? -->
<p>Object: {{ product }}</p>   <!-- Shows [object Object] - not useful -->
```

